1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,840
Johannesburg. It's month-end, and the
payday queue snakes out of the shop door.

2
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:08,960
Let's call the man near the front Tendai.

3
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:10,800
He builds houses for a living,

4
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:16,160
and today he's sending one thousand two
hundred rand home to his mother in Bulawayo.

5
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:21,200
He counts the notes twice, slides them
under the glass, and takes his slip.

6
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:26,640
But by the time that money reaches her,
a slice of it has already gone.

7
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:30,960
That slice is bigger here
than almost anywhere else.

8
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:32,880
According to the World Bank,

9
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:37,760
South Africa is the most expensive
country in the G20 to send money from.

10
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:40,560
So where does that money go?

11
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:44,120
And why does it cost so
much to send it from here?

12
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:47,680
Let's follow Tendai's
rand all the way home.

13
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:50,080
First, how big is the slice?

14
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:55,040
The World Bank checks the price of sending
money in hundreds of corridors around the

15
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:57,760
world. In late 2024,

16
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:02,000
sending money from South Africa
cost twelve percent on average.

17
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:04,840
The world average was
about six and a half.

18
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:06,400
Think of it like this.

19
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:12,320
Out of every one hundred rand sent, about
twelve rand never reaches the family.

20
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:13,760
At that average,

21
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:19,200
Tendai's one thousand two hundred rand
loses about one hundred and forty rand.

22
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:21,200
If he sends that every month,

23
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:25,120
it adds up to more than one
thousand seven hundred rand a year.

24
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:28,240
On the road from South Africa to Zimbabwe,

25
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:31,360
the average was twelve
point seven percent.

26
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:34,880
The world's goal is three percent by 2030.

27
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:38,080
South Africa is four times over it.

28
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:40,960
And the people paying it are not the rich.

29
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,200
They are builders, cleaners,
drivers and shop workers.

30
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:46,720
People like Tendai,

31
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:52,000
and many of the people who top up phones
with us at Fluxr, every single day.

32
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:53,920
More on that in a minute.

33
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:56,800
So how much money are we talking about?

34
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:02,320
In 2024, people in South Africa sent
more than nineteen billion rand to other

35
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:06,480
countries in southern Africa,
through formal channels alone.

36
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:10,240
That was fifteen point
seven million transfers.

37
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:15,520
Zimbabwe got the biggest share by far:
eleven point eight billion rand,

38
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:18,400
in eight and a half million transfers.

39
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:21,120
And most of those transfers are small.

40
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:25,120
Most sit between five hundred
and nineteen hundred rand.

41
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:27,280
Exactly like Tendai's.

42
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:30,160
For Zimbabwe, this money matters.

43
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:35,920
In 2023, money sent home from abroad
was nine point six percent of the whole

44
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,640
economy. So why is something
this important so expensive?

45
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:43,307
There are five reasons.

46
00:02:43,333 --> 00:02:47,200
And the biggest one is the one
you never see on the receipt.

47
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:50,080
Reason number one: who you send with.

48
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:53,040
In late 2024, worldwide,

49
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:56,240
banks were the most
expensive way to send money,

50
00:02:56,320 --> 00:02:59,520
at thirteen point six percent on average.

51
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:02,880
Money transfer companies
were about five point four.

52
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:06,320
Mobile phone operators,
about two point eight.

53
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:09,680
So the same transfer can
cost very different amounts,

54
00:03:09,880 --> 00:03:11,680
depending on where you walk in.

55
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:14,800
Reason number two: the paperwork.

56
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:18,640
To send money formally,
you need the right documents.

57
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:23,680
South Africa's rules ask banks and transfer
companies to check a customer's identity

58
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:27,440
and status, and to report
on their transfers.

59
00:03:27,520 --> 00:03:31,520
The IMF and the World Bank say
those requirements are heavy,

60
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:34,360
compared to how small most transfers are.

61
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:36,507
Every check costs money.

62
00:03:36,533 --> 00:03:38,800
And it's built into the price.

63
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:41,600
Reason number three: cash.

64
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:44,533
Most of this money starts as cash.

65
00:03:44,587 --> 00:03:47,720
On the busiest routes,
like Zimbabwe and Malawi,

66
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:52,600
eighty to ninety percent of transfers
are paid in cash at the counter.

67
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:57,880
Cash has to be counted, guarded and
moved, at both ends of the journey.

68
00:03:57,920 --> 00:04:01,173
Which brings us to the
biggest reason of all.

69
00:04:01,227 --> 00:04:03,440
But first, a quick word from us.

70
00:04:04,313 --> 00:04:09,193
If you're in South Africa, you can send
airtime and data home with Fluxr.

71
00:04:09,313 --> 00:04:13,233
Buy a 1Voucher, OTT Voucher, Blu Voucher,

72
00:04:13,433 --> 00:04:17,593
FNB Voucher or 4ALL
Voucher at your local store,

73
00:04:17,673 --> 00:04:23,273
then dial star one three zero,
star three one zero two six, star,

74
00:04:23,353 --> 00:04:25,193
your voucher PIN, hash.

75
00:04:25,433 --> 00:04:27,993
Top up your own phone
here in South Africa,

76
00:04:28,073 --> 00:04:30,873
or send airtime and data
to family back home.

77
00:04:31,193 --> 00:04:34,153
It works on any phone, no data needed.

78
00:04:34,473 --> 00:04:39,353
Or get the Fluxr app on Google Play:
turn your voucher into wallet credit,

79
00:04:39,513 --> 00:04:44,153
then buy airtime, data and
electricity, here and back home,

80
00:04:44,313 --> 00:04:47,513
plus vouchers and bill
payments, all in one app.

81
00:04:48,153 --> 00:04:50,073
Now, back to Tendai's money.

82
00:04:50,633 --> 00:04:55,593
Reason number four, the one
you never see: the last mile.

83
00:04:55,913 --> 00:05:01,073
When Tendai's money lands in Zimbabwe,
his mother still needs it in her hand.

84
00:05:01,353 --> 00:05:07,033
Someone has to bring cash to a counter in
Bulawayo, keep it safe, and pay it out.

85
00:05:07,113 --> 00:05:10,313
The IMF and the World Bank
found that in Zimbabwe,

86
00:05:10,393 --> 00:05:14,753
cash makes up more than half of the
whole cost of sending money home.

87
00:05:15,033 --> 00:05:18,953
Cash in, cash out, security and transport.

88
00:05:19,513 --> 00:05:22,433
And the cost isn't only
the fee on the slip.

89
00:05:22,713 --> 00:05:25,353
Part of it hides in the exchange rate,

90
00:05:25,393 --> 00:05:28,473
the rate you get when your
rand turns into dollars.

91
00:05:28,713 --> 00:05:32,393
Here's how. The company
buys dollars at one price,

92
00:05:32,433 --> 00:05:35,233
and sells them to you
at a slightly worse one.

93
00:05:35,513 --> 00:05:37,753
That gap is called the margin.

94
00:05:38,233 --> 00:05:42,393
It's not printed on the slip as a
fee, but the World Bank counts it,

95
00:05:42,473 --> 00:05:45,073
because the family at home feels it.

96
00:05:45,513 --> 00:05:48,633
Reason number five: too few players.

97
00:05:49,193 --> 00:05:52,553
A handful of transfer companies
carry most of this money.

98
00:05:52,793 --> 00:05:56,073
And many South African banks
are wary of working with them,

99
00:05:56,193 --> 00:05:58,153
because of the compliance risk.

100
00:05:58,233 --> 00:06:00,153
In February 2023,

101
00:06:00,233 --> 00:06:04,953
South Africa was even put on the global
grey list for financial crime risks,

102
00:06:05,033 --> 00:06:06,953
which brought extra checks.

103
00:06:07,033 --> 00:06:10,153
Fewer players means
less pressure on price.

104
00:06:10,393 --> 00:06:14,073
And that pushes some people to
another way of sending money.

105
00:06:15,037 --> 00:06:19,157
At bus ranks across South Africa,
you can see it every week.

106
00:06:19,437 --> 00:06:21,477
Parcels wrapped for Zimbabwe.

107
00:06:21,757 --> 00:06:25,117
Blankets, groceries,
and envelopes of cash,

108
00:06:25,157 --> 00:06:27,917
handed to a driver or a
passenger heading home.

109
00:06:28,477 --> 00:06:30,477
People call it hand to hand.

110
00:06:30,957 --> 00:06:33,437
It needs no documents, and no bank.

111
00:06:33,917 --> 00:06:37,397
But if the money goes missing,
there's no one to call.

112
00:06:37,837 --> 00:06:42,757
Back in 2017, a woman in Cape Town
told reporters how she does it.

113
00:06:43,037 --> 00:06:47,677
She goes to the long-distance bus terminal,
finds someone travelling to Harare,

114
00:06:47,757 --> 00:06:49,917
and hands the money over in person.

115
00:06:50,557 --> 00:06:55,677
A 2024 study backed by the Reserve Bank
estimated that about three point four

116
00:06:55,917 --> 00:06:58,557
billion rand a year moves this way,

117
00:06:58,597 --> 00:07:01,517
and said the real number
is probably much higher.

118
00:07:02,237 --> 00:07:03,917
So is anything changing?

119
00:07:04,637 --> 00:07:05,997
Yes, slowly.

120
00:07:06,637 --> 00:07:12,557
In early 2021, sending from South Africa
cost almost sixteen percent on average.

121
00:07:13,037 --> 00:07:15,757
By late 2024, it was twelve.

122
00:07:16,477 --> 00:07:20,957
In October 2025, South Africa
came off the grey list.

123
00:07:21,277 --> 00:07:25,837
Banks across southern Africa are being
linked by a regional instant-payment system.

124
00:07:26,397 --> 00:07:31,437
Since October 2025, it has carried
payments from South Africa to Zimbabwe,

125
00:07:31,677 --> 00:07:32,677
around the clock.

126
00:07:33,197 --> 00:07:35,437
And digital sending is cheaper.

127
00:07:35,757 --> 00:07:41,277
Worldwide, sending money digitally
cost about five percent in late 2024,

128
00:07:41,357 --> 00:07:43,677
against more than seven percent for cash.

129
00:07:44,397 --> 00:07:48,717
So if you send money home,
check the total, not just the fee.

130
00:07:49,277 --> 00:07:54,157
Ask how much will arrive on the other side,
in the other currency, before you pay.

131
00:07:54,637 --> 00:07:57,197
Because for millions
of people like Tendai,

132
00:07:57,277 --> 00:08:00,397
the slice is still far
bigger than three percent.

133
00:08:01,357 --> 00:08:02,357
Let's recap.

134
00:08:02,797 --> 00:08:08,077
South Africa is the most expensive
country in the G20 to send money from:

135
00:08:08,157 --> 00:08:13,477
about twelve rand of every hundred,
against about six and a half worldwide.

136
00:08:13,837 --> 00:08:16,477
Why? Banks cost the most.

137
00:08:16,717 --> 00:08:18,477
The paperwork is heavy.

138
00:08:18,797 --> 00:08:23,117
Most transfers start as cash,
and many end as cash.

139
00:08:23,437 --> 00:08:25,117
Too few companies compete.

140
00:08:25,677 --> 00:08:28,837
And part of the price
hides in the exchange rate.

141
00:08:29,277 --> 00:08:31,477
It's getting cheaper, slowly.

142
00:08:31,757 --> 00:08:34,957
But until sending money
home costs what it should,

143
00:08:35,037 --> 00:08:37,597
every rand that arrives still counts.

144
00:08:38,477 --> 00:08:41,677
If you enjoyed this story,
subscribe for the next one.

145
00:08:41,917 --> 00:08:45,597
And if you're in South Africa,
sending airtime and data home,

146
00:08:45,677 --> 00:08:47,197
you know where to find us.

